<!--meta {"lead": "Themes change what a role looks like, never which role a component asks for. Light and dark ship today; the same mechanism carries a partner or high-contrast theme.", "description": "How light and dark themes work, the three token tiers, adding a theme, contrast preferences and tokens.json.", "covers": "data-theme and prefers-color-scheme, token tiers, adding a theme, contrast and forced colours, tokens.json"} -->
<section class="ds-section" id="overview"><h2><a href="#overview">Overview</a></h2>
  <p>All colour and elevation in HairPop is read through semantic tokens such as <code>--color-bg-surface</code> or <code>--color-fg-accent</code>. A theme is a block in <code>tokens/tokens.css</code> that gives those tokens different values. Components, pages and the mocks never know which theme is active.</p>
  {{themes}}<div class="card stack" style="flex: 1 1 14rem;"><p class="eyebrow">Available Sat 24 Oct</p><h3 class="card__title">Nia Okafor</h3><p class="text-muted">4.9 from 86 reviews · from $180</p><span class="badge badge--warning">Awaiting reply</span><button class="btn btn--primary btn--sm" type="button">Request with Nia</button></div>{{/themes}}
</section>

<section class="ds-section" id="how"><h2><a href="#how">How themes are applied</a></h2>
  <ol>
    <li><b>Light</b> is the default. Its themed tokens live in <code>:root, [data-theme="light"]</code>, so a light preview nested inside a dark page (like the left panel above) re-declares them. Layout, type and spacing tokens stay on bare <code>:root</code> and keep inheriting.</li>
    <li><b>Dark</b> applies with <code>[data-theme="dark"]</code> on any element, usually <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>.</li>
    <li><b>Operating-system preference</b>: the same dark values sit inside <code>@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { :root:not([data-theme="light"]) { … } }</code>, so a person who prefers dark gets it unless the product forces light. The two dark blocks must stay identical; copy one into the other when you change either. <code>check_contrast.py</code> reads both.</li>
    <li><b><code>color-scheme</code></b> is set in each theme so native controls, scrollbars and autofill follow.</li>
  </ol>
  <pre><code>&lt;html lang="en"&gt;                    &lt;!-- follows the OS --&gt;
&lt;html lang="en" data-theme="dark"&gt;  &lt;!-- forced dark (saved preference) --&gt;
&lt;html lang="en" data-theme="light"&gt; &lt;!-- forced light --&gt;</code></pre>
  <p>Persist a person's explicit choice and set the attribute before first paint to avoid a flash. The documentation pages honour <code>?theme=dark</code>, and the <kbd>t</kbd> key toggles.</p>
  <h3>Dark is designed, not inverted</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Surfaces get lighter as they rise: canvas (grey 950) &lt; surface (grey 900) &lt; raised (grey 800). Dialogs read as raised because they are lighter, not only because of a shadow.</li>
    <li>The plum accent lightens to plum 300 and text on it flips to plum 950; danger does the same with red.</li>
    <li>Status backgrounds become deep tints (green 950, amber 950, red 950) with light text.</li>
    <li>Shadows are darker and more opaque, because a soft grey shadow disappears on a dark canvas.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="ds-section" id="tiers"><h2><a href="#tiers">Token tiers</a></h2>
  <div class="ds-table-wrap"><table class="ds-table">
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Tier</th><th scope="col">Prefix</th><th scope="col">Example</th><th scope="col">Who reads it</th><th scope="col">Who changes it</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Primitive</td><td><code>--palette-*</code>, <code>--font-*</code></td><td><code>--palette-plum-600</code></td><td>Semantic tokens only</td><td>A brand refresh</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Semantic</td><td><code>--color-*</code>, <code>--text-*</code>, <code>--space-*</code>, <code>--radius-*</code>, <code>--shadow-*</code>, <code>--duration-*</code>, <code>--z-*</code>, <code>--layout-*</code></td><td><code>--color-accent: var(--palette-plum-600)</code></td><td>Components and pages</td><td>A theme</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Component</td><td><code>--btn-*</code>, <code>--field-*</code>, <code>--card-*</code>, <code>--badge-*</code>, <code>--dialog-*</code>, <code>--toast-*</code></td><td><code>--btn-bg: var(--color-accent)</code></td><td>One component</td><td>A local re-skin</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <pre><code>/* primitive */ --palette-plum-600: …;
/* semantic  */ --color-accent: var(--palette-plum-600);         /* dark: var(--palette-plum-300) */
/* component */ .btn--primary { --btn-bg: var(--color-accent); }</code></pre>
</section>

<section class="ds-section" id="adding"><h2><a href="#adding">Adding a theme</a></h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Add any new primitives the theme needs to the primitive section of <code>tokens.css</code>.</li>
    <li>Add a <code>[data-theme="&lt;name&gt;"]</code> block that overrides <b>semantic tokens only</b>. Start by copying the light block's colour section; set <code>color-scheme</code>.</li>
    <li>Run <code>check_contrast.py tokens/tokens.css</code>; every pair in <code>contrast-pairs.json</code> must pass for the new theme too.</li>
    <li>Run <code>tokens_to_json.py</code> to refresh <code>tokens.json</code>, and add the theme to the screenshot matrix.</li>
  </ol>
  <pre><code>/* A partner salon theme: teal accent, everything else inherited */
[data-theme="partner"] {
  --color-accent: var(--palette-teal-700);
  --color-accent-hover: var(--palette-teal-800);
  --color-fg-accent: var(--palette-teal-800);
  --color-focus-ring: var(--palette-teal-700);
  color-scheme: light;
}</code></pre>
</section>

<section class="ds-section" id="preferences"><h2><a href="#preferences">Contrast and forced colours</a></h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b><code>prefers-contrast: more</code></b> raises decorative borders to <code>--color-border-strong</code> and muted and subtle text one step towards <code>--color-fg-default</code>.</li>
    <li><b><code>forced-colors: active</code></b> (Windows High Contrast) hands the focus ring to <code>Highlight</code> and borders to <code>CanvasText</code>; components add a system-colour border so cards, chips and inputs keep their edges, and selected nav links, tabs and chips get a <code>Highlight</code> outline.</li>
    <li><b><code>prefers-reduced-motion</code></b> is handled by the motion tokens; see <a href="motion.html">Motion</a>.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="ds-section" id="json"><h2><a href="#json">Consuming tokens.json</a></h2>
  <p><a href="../tokens/tokens.json">tokens/tokens.json</a> is generated from <code>tokens.css</code> by <code>tokens_to_json.py</code> in the W3C Design Tokens (DTCG) format, for native apps, email templates and anything that cannot read CSS.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Each token has <code>$type</code> and <code>$value</code>; aliases are written <code>{palette.plum-600}</code> so the tier relationship survives.</li>
    <li>Dark values sit under <code>$extensions</code> on the same token, so a platform build resolves both themes from one file.</li>
    <li>Never edit <code>tokens.json</code> by hand; change <code>tokens.css</code> and regenerate.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="ds-section" id="usage"><h2><a href="#usage">Usage</a></h2>
  <div class="ds-dodont">
    <figure class="ds-do"><div class="ds-example__canvas"><code>color: var(--color-fg-muted);</code></div><figcaption>Read colour by role, so the value follows the theme.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="ds-dont"><div class="ds-example__canvas"><code>color: var(--palette-gray-600);</code></div><figcaption>Read a primitive in a component: it stays mid-grey on a dark surface and fails contrast.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="ds-do"><div class="ds-example__canvas"><code>.promo .btn--primary { --btn-bg: … }</code></div><figcaption>Re-skin one place by overriding component tokens.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="ds-dont"><div class="ds-example__canvas"><code>[data-theme="dark"] .btn { background: … }</code></div><figcaption>Write per-theme component rules; the semantic tier already does this.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="ds-section" id="accessibility"><h2><a href="#accessibility">Accessibility</a></h2>
  <p>Every theme meets WCAG 2.2 AA for every pairing in <code>tokens/contrast-pairs.json</code>: 4.5:1 for text and 3:1 for control edges, focus rings and meaningful icons. The <a href="color.html">colour</a> page shows each pair with its live ratio; switch the theme to see the other values. A theme that fails any pair is not shipped.</p>
</section>
